  "slack read": {
    "help": "exit 0
Read a channel, existing DM, or thread without advancing the read cursor.

USAGE
  teamhub slack read <#name> [--thread <id>] [flags]
  teamhub slack read (--channel-id <id> | --user <email> | --user-id <id> | --message-url <url>) [flags]
  teamhub slack read --schema [--json]

FLAGS
  --limit <value>               1–100 items per page; default 20.
  --cursor <value>              Cursor from the previous page; keep the same account and query.
  --channel-id <value>          Conversation ID; skip name discovery.
  --user <value>                Full email; read looks up an existing DM, send may create one.
  --user-id <value>             User ID; skip email lookup.
  --message-url <value>         Slack HTTPS message URL.
  --thread <value>              Parent message ID of the thread in that conversation.
  --account <value>             Select a connection ID or complete account label; when authorization is missing, an authorization card is shown in the chat.
  --json                        Print complete JSON instead of the default readable text.
  --schema                      Show offline input and output schemas without business arguments.

INHERITED FLAGS
  -h, --help                   Show help.

OUTPUT
  Shows the actual account, plus Message or thread bodies, authors, files, and next-page cursor.

Help and schema are available offline; TeamHub checks current access at execution time.

EXAMPLES
  teamhub slack read '#engineering'
  teamhub slack read --user alice@example.com

LEARN MORE
  teamhub slack --help
  teamhub slack read --schema --json
